    {
      "family": "017",
      "verdict": "bears",
      "title": "The irrationality exponent of π is 2",
      "leanDoc": "lean/docs/017.md",
      "records": [
        {
          "id": "7a",
          "tag": "formal-proof",
          "statementChecked": true,
          "familyTitle": "The irrationality exponent of π is 2",
          "claim": "that the irrationality measure of π is exactly 2.",
          "statement": {
            "path": "lean/ComparatorChallenges/PiExponent.lean",
            "theorem": "main",
            "text": "theorem main :\n  (∀ ν : ℝ, 2 < ν → ∃ Q : ℤ, 2 ≤ Q ∧\n    ∀ p q : ℤ, Q ≤ q →\n      (q : ℝ) ^ (-ν) ≤ |Real.pi - (p : ℝ) / (q : ℝ)|) ∧\n  sSup {ν : ℝ | 0 < ν ∧\n    Set.Infinite {r : ℚ | 2 ≤ r.den ∧\n      0 < |Real.pi - (r : ℝ)| ∧\n      |Real.pi - (r : ℝ)| < (r.den : ℝ) ^ (-ν)}} = 2"
          },
          "match": "Same quantity: the first half says that for every ν > 2, |π − p/q| ≥ q^(−ν) once q is large enough, and the second that the supremum of exponents with infinitely many such approximations is 2. That makes this constant 2, against 7.103205334137 in its last-listed upper-bound row.",
          "screenedBy": [
            "claude",
            "codex"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
